# std/mem/ctx — scope-provided data (context.md): how a scope makes typed values available to its
# dynamic extent without threading parameters, and how consumers reach them by naming a type. This
# generalizes the convention the language already runs on — memory_model's ambient node, the hidden
# first argument every `!` function receives, is the primordial context entry; `provide`/`consume`
# extend the same hidden-argument convention to user data. Each provided type becomes one more
# comptime-planned hidden argument to exactly the functions that demand it (context.md §4). Nothing
# here is a runtime registry: provision, discharge, and routing are all compile-time, and the only
# runtime artifact is a pointer in a register.
#
# Two effects, two orthogonal colors (context.md §1): `!` needs the ambient node (may reserve
# memory); `uses T` needs a hidden `T` pointer (reads the nearest provide of T). Providing IS
# allocating — the provide site places the aggregate survivor-side in the pipe's carved arena — so
# `provide` lives on the `!` side; consuming reads through a threaded pointer and allocates nothing.
#
# Both are bodyless intrinsics in the style of the geometry constructors (geometry_lowering §1): the
# user imports the token and the compiler supplies provision, discharge, and the routing at the call
# site. A provided type carries DATA ONLY — no fn-typed field, transitively (context.md §3), checked
# and rejected by the demand pass.

# `ctx::provide(x)` — a construction-pipe stage, data-last (the stdlib rule): the user supplies the
# value `x`, the pipe supplies the freshly carved node as the trailing argument. The aggregate is
# placed survivor-side in that node and the node threads on, so
#
#   std::mem::growing_arena::of(1024)
#       |> std::mem::ctx::provide(config)
#       |> defer std::mem::growing_arena::destroy
#
# reads as "this scope provides `config`, reclaimed at exit". Extent is lexical — from the provide
# statement's position to the end of the enclosing scope (context.md §2). The provided type joins
# the geometry's comptime shape alongside fixed-vs-elastic and the chunk size.
pub intrinsic provide = ['T]('T value, Uarch node): Uarch

# `ctx::consume(T)` — a comptime binder naming the hidden `T` argument: no runtime call, no copy.
# A read compiles to a load through the threaded pointer:
#
#   const { sentry_host } = std::mem::ctx::consume(SentryConfig)
#
# The enclosing function gains `T` as a demand, propagated bottom-up over the call tree by the same
# lattice that colors `!`; a `pub` consumer must declare it with a `uses T` clause (context.md §4).
# A demand no provide in the consumer's lexical extent can discharge is a comptime error.
pub intrinsic consume = ['T](): 'T
